Translated Content 4:
IV. The man who was seen in the house, grey-haired and elderly,
He too rose to his feet, like a powerful mountain tiger.
A stranger come as a guest, he respects the guest of another;
To stand up is the law, and he cannot break the law of the mountains.
But when he saw the stranger, his face took on the hue of a wolf;
It was easy to discern what he was thinking.
The elderly Kist did not relish the sight of the foreign youth.
His heart beats overcome with rage, and his face does not hide it;
His hand reaches for his dagger, quietly testing the blade,
But a guest in another's house cannot stir up trouble.
He rose and went out silently, biting his finger bitterly;
Three times he struck his hand against his heart as he stepped outside.

Translated Content 21:
The sons of the Kists were stirred, everyone buckled on their swords;
The whole village was in turmoil: child, man, and woman.
They must sacrifice Zviadauri’s head to their dead;
They must slaughter him upon the grave, as is the custom.

Translated Content 0:
I returned from the graveyard, I was searching for your horse in the ravines,
Instead of the horse, wretched me, I stumbled right into the devis.
One met me, clad in a black felt cloak, of an enormous build:
Still before my eyes flashes the foulness of his skin;
Big-eared, teeth-gnashing, hideous, black in color;
He reached out his hands to me, hands as large as a head,
A blackish leather hat on his head appeared the size of a mountain.
He told me: "Come with me, live with me, woman,
I have much gold and silver, I shall hide nothing from you."
I fled, I was terrified, the devi pursued me with a roar;
The whole earth shook with a rumble beneath his feet;
This sound chilled me, as mountains and plains spun to and fro,
I barely reached here to tell you of my woe with a rasping breath.

