They sat on the flat roof and feasted, drinking it from bowls;
Some played the panduri, singing loudly over it,
Laying heroic tales as a bridge for the listeners' feelings;
With excitement, they recited the names of heroes sacredly in verses;
The elders smoked pipes, smoke wrapped around them like mist,
They explained their stories, offering blessings for the heroes.
To instruct the young ones, they recalled them with praise:
“It is up to you, whoever of you will prove to be a man from now on!”

Translated Content 12:
In the assembly, one appears, pale-faced and calm;
Like insects to honey, young and old swarm around him.
A sword is girded at his somber waist, a shield hangs from his shoulder.
Before his knees, two bareheaded men sit kneeling;
They offer him bowls, drawing black beer from a large wooden basin.
“Drink, Mindia,” they say, “it is good beer, hoppy;
Tell us something, by God’s grace, something Georgian and wise.”

Translated Content 0:
Swift as a bullet, mystical as a snake,
All of Pshav-Khevsureti deifies him and sees him as solace,
The crowned Tamar is always saying this:
“As long as I have Mindia with me, and with him the mountain folk,
No matter how hard the enemy tries, they can do me no harm.”
He knows the tricks to defeat and crush the enemy;
If he reaches the wounded while they still live, let them have no fear of death.
His peerless medicine will heal even one cut in two;
Thus his numbers do not dwindle in wars, he always has a vast army;
The tale of his adventures has traveled far and wide.

